ant that we all understand that it  encompasses both the microbes and the  environment and their function  altogether  the other interesting and important  aspect to consider here is you know the  fact that we now really consider  ourselves or view ourselves in the field  as a hollow biome and what do I mean by  that that means that we as humans human  beings have our own host genome we have  the genes that influence our biology and  our structure and function but we  understand now I think more than ever  that we're colonized both within and on  our bodies with these microorganisms  that bring their own genome to this  um this consorture this collaboration  and that together is is what we're  talking about when we say the holobion  the the microorganisms and ourselves and  all the interactions that those genes  and influences have together and then we  have of course the environment in which  we live that also dictates how that  holobions exists in in the in the  environment they're in so if you live in  a very moist rainy hydrated environment  versus living in the desert the  organisms and the environment that you  reside in is going to info influence  some of the interactions in the  organisms that you might be associated  with as well so it's important to have  this perspective as we talk about the  skin because we're going to talk about  it in a holistic way meaning both us as  human beings and the organisms that live  on that skin itself  so first let's talk about the microbiota  in the context of the skin microbiome so  on the skin which I'll explain in more  detail more about the structured  function in a few slides what we have  what we Now understand really is that we  have a balanced microbiome on the skin  of of our of our of our human body and  it's a habitat of billions of beneficial  and potentially harmful bacteria that  all exist in this balance in a state of  health and an imbalance of these  organisms or an imbalance in the  environment I.E the sk